// FLYBY — a 3D flying game written in Milo.
//
//   milo build examples/games/flight/main.milo -o /tmp/flyby --release && /tmp/flyby
//   /tmp/flyby --help
//
// San Francisco is baked into the binary (see `usage` below), so the game ships
// as one file with no asset path to get wrong.
//
// Real 3D, all of it on the GPU: gfx3d.milo records world-space triangles into
// vertex streams, gpuworld.milo hands them to the driver, and gpupost.milo does
// the sea, the 2D overlay, the bloom and the tone map. The sky, the distance haze
// and the sea reflection all call one `skyColor` (sky.milo), which is what makes
// them agree.
//
// It needs an OpenGL 3.3 core context and there is no software fallback. The one
// that used to exist is now benchmarks/softraster — a benchmark, not a renderer.
//
// You cannot crash. The ground pushes you up if you dive into it.

